            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Impact on Mathematics and Philosophy</strong><br>\nGödel's work had a profound effect on how mathematicians and philosophers view the foundations of mathematics. His theorems suggested that mathematical truths could exist beyond formal proof, thus implying that intuition and discovery play a role in mathematics. Gödel's ideas continue to influence the philosophical debates about the nature of mathematical truths.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Later Life and Mental Health</strong><br>\nIn his later life, Gödel suffered from mental health issues, including paranoia and depression. Despite his struggles, he continued to contribute to mathematics, although he did not produce work as significant as his earlier theorems. His health issues affected his teaching and research, leading to a period of isolation.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Relationship with Einstein</strong><br>\nGödel had a close friendship with Albert Einstein when they both worked at the Institute for Advanced Study in Princeton. Einstein admired Gödel's intellect and once remarked that his own work no longer mattered and that he only went to the institute \"just to have the privilege of walking home with Gödel.\"</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
