          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Gödel’s Philosophical Views</strong><br>\nGödel held strong philosophical views, influenced by his study of Leibniz, Kant, and Husserl. He believed in an objective mathematical reality that could be accessed through rigorous logical thought. This philosophical stance shaped his approach to logic and mathematics, where he searched for absolute truths.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal Life and Habits</strong><br>\nGödel was known for his meticulous and precise habits, which were evident in both his work and personal life. He was fastidious about his diet and health, often to the point of obsession. Gödel's dedication to his work and his personal routines were interlinked with his quest for order and certainty.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Death</strong><br>\nGödel left behind a legacy as one of the most significant logicians in history. His work provided crucial insights into the limitations of mathematical systems and the nature of proof. He died on January 14, 1978, from complications related to malnutrition, after his wife's illness prevented her from cooking for him, underscoring the depth of his reliance on routine. His intellectual contributions continue to resonate in the fields of mathematics, computer science, and philosophy.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
